Title: Magnolia Farm
Order: 2
Production Code: 1x02

INT. WHITE EAGLE'S ISLAND, THE COTTAGE - NIGHT

PAGE: 1

PANEL: Straight on from the last page: the kitchen, the dead man on the skins, Warren with his hand at the wound, Maybee with the lantern, Judah in the doorway, old Nokomis on her knees. Rain against the glass.

NOKOMIS
It is a mistake. Who would kill White Eagle? There lives not an Indian in the whole country round who does not love him.

PANEL: Close on Maybee, bewildered, wiping his face.

MAYBEE
Blame my skin! Where's the blud?

PANEL: Warren half-raises the body and shows the bullet hole in the back; the blood has pooled inside, under the shirt.

WARREN
Look! Internal hemorrhage.

PANEL: Maybee drops his lantern hand, appalled.

MAYBEE
By the Etarn'l! Must 'a bled to death whilst we was comin'.

PAGE: 2

PANEL: Judah on his knees beside the bed of skins, both hands on his father's chest.

JUDAH
God in heaven! It is true! But who has done it? No one lives here but ourselves. Murdered! My father! My master!

PANEL: Maybee, steady now, one big hand on the boy's shoulder.

MAYBEE
Hush. 'Tain't no time fer cryin' nor makin' a fuss. Tell us all you know about this business.

PANEL: Judah, hollow-eyed, tells it, hands open.

JUDAH
He went out after supper to look after the canoes. In a short time we heard a shout and then a cry, "Help!

JUDAH (CONT'D)
help!" and we ran to him, Winona and I.

PANEL: Flashback, grey-green: White Eagle leaning against a giant pine, one hand pressed to his side, Judah and Winona running up through the dark, lightning behind.

WHITE EAGLE
Get me to the house; get a doctor, I am hurt.

PANEL: Back in the room. Warren, tight-lipped.

WARREN
A doctor must be summoned to certify the cause of death, and the police authorities must be notified.

PAGE: 3

PANEL: Maybee pulling on his coat at the door with Judah. Both with lanterns.

MAYBEE
I'll take the boat and Judah here, and be back by sunrise.

PANEL: Judah lingers, giving Warren a wistful look.

PANEL: Warren puts his hand on the boy's shoulder.

WARREN
Yes. I will tell her.

PANEL: Wide on the shore: two lantern points moving off across black water.

PANEL: The bedroom. Winona on the bed where he laid her, long hair lank with the rain, lips slightly parted, unconscious; Nokomis at her side. Warren in the doorway looking at her for a long moment.

CAPTION: As he remembered her story and the position that her father's death placed her in, his soul went out to her in infinite pity.

PAGE: 4

PANEL: Warren sits beside the bed as Winona opens her eyes. He is gentle, careful; she hears him with a stunned face, pale lips, strained eyes.

WARREN
You shall not be friendless if I can help you.

PANEL: Winona sits bolt upright, the cry torn out of her.

WINONA
My father! Oh, my father! Murdered!

PANEL: She tries to rush from the room; Warren holds her, she fights him with small fingers, almost fierce. Nokomis wrings her hands.

PANEL: The paroxysm passes; she sags in his arms, exhausted; her hand still clutching his sleeve in bands of steel.

PANEL: The window: the first grey light and a rain-washed sky.

CAPTION: The great storm had cleared the air of the electric heat.

PAGE: 5

EXT. WHITE EAGLE'S ISLAND - MORNING

PANEL: Wide: a gloriously beautiful morning. The island's maples and hemlock rich in sunshine, the lake rippling as if no storm had been. In the foreground, Maybee's boat grounding with six men, officers and townsfolk.

CAPTION: Mr. Maybee returned at sunrise, bringing men with him.

PANEL: Men fan out through the woods and along the beach, looking down, kneeling, shaking their heads. The storm has erased everything.

CAPTION: Search revealed no trace of the assassin; no clue.

PANEL: The cottage: Warren talks to a police officer, who defers to him; Maybee confides in him; Winona on the porch holds Warren's sleeve in slender brown fingers. Two men at the edge of the search party, THOMSON and the LEADER (Colonel Titus), hats in hand, serious, helpful. The Leader's eyes do not meet anyone's.

CAPTION: There were but two strangers in the city who had visited the island, and they immediately joined the searchers.

PANEL: Inside, the strongbox: the dead man's trunk opened, shirts and a few trinkets; Nokomis shakes her head. Nothing missing, no scrap of paper.

CAPTION: There was not even a scrap of paper found to tell who White Eagle might have been in earlier, happier days.

PAGE: 6

PANEL: Nokomis takes Warren's hands in both her withered ones.

NOKOMIS
Surely it was the Great Father must have sent you to us.

PANEL: Judah, walking alone along the beach at dusk, staring at nothing; his jaw set.

CAPTION: Judah walked about all day with a dazed expression, crying silently but bitterly, and a growing look of sullen fury.

PANEL: Close on his lips as the words form.

JUDAH
I'll find him when I'm older if he's on top of the earth,

JUDAH (CONT'D)
and then it'll be him or me who will lie as my poor master lies in there to-day.

PANEL: Wide: the funeral. A great pine, the grave dug beneath it; Indians in blankets and buckskin gathered from towns on both shores; Winona, small and straight, in front.

CAPTION: They buried him beneath the giant pine against which he was found, wounded to death.

PANEL: At the edge of the crowd, white onlookers drawn by curiosity; among them the two strangers, hats off, Thomson grave, the Leader staring at the grave.

PAGE: 7

INT. GRAND ISLAND HOTEL, WARREN'S ROOM - DAY

PANEL: Two days later. Warren and Maybee sit in the room; Maybee with his boots on the bed, a pipe, a smile.

MAYBEE
You've been a friend in need, sir, durn me ef you ain't.

PANEL: Warren puts the question seriously, leaning forward.

WARREN
What is to become of Winona after I leave this place? It is different with the boy. He can manage somehow; but the girl.

PANEL: Maybee considers, holding his pipe in his fist.

MAYBEE
She'll never have to say she's got no friend while Ebenezer Maybee's proprietor of the Grand Island Ho-tel.

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
My wife's plum crazy to git that young kidabid.

PANEL: Maybee, wry, with an arm out toward the window and the stable yard beyond.

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
Neither of them two forlorn critters realizes what "bein' a nigger" means. God knows I pity 'em.

PANEL: Warren, resolved, a hand flat on the table.

WARREN
Mr. Maybee, I have determined to write home and see if something can't be done to educate these children.

WARREN (CONT'D)
In England, neither their color nor race will be against them.

PAGE: 8

PANEL: Maybee, open-mouthed, his pipe dropped on the blanket.

MAYBEE
Yer jokin'?

PANEL: Warren, grave and sure.

WARREN
No, I mean it. I wish you to take charge of them. I am called to Virginia on important business.

WARREN (CONT'D)
When I return, I shall be able to tell just what I can do.

PANEL: Maybee grasps his hand hard in both of his.

MAYBEE
Mr. Maxwell, you're a white man. I jes' froze to you, I did, the fus' night you poked yer head in the door.

PANEL: The two men's clasped hands, big and small, over a table with the visiting card still on it.

WARREN
And I to you.

MAYBEE
Don' you ever be skeery whilst yer in Amerika an' Ebenezer Maybee's on top o' the earth.

EXT. WHITE EAGLE'S ISLAND, THE GRAVE - AFTERNOON

PANEL: The tall pine; Winona sits beside the new-made grave, hands folded listlessly in her lap, eyes on the sunlit waves.

PANEL: She turns as Warren comes up the slope, and a sudden radiance crosses her whole face.

WINONA
Ah! It is you. I have been waiting you.

PAGE: 9

PANEL: They sit side by side on the roots, Warren holding her hand.

WARREN
I could not go to England and leave you here alone and friendless, Winona. I could not bear it.

PANEL: Winona, sorrow having matured her wonderfully, looks at the lake.

WINONA
Papa never spoke of any friends. He used to say that we two had only each other to love, poor papa.

PANEL: Warren, quiet and kind, explains with an open hand.

WARREN
I will take you to England. And Judah.

PANEL: Winona, with a long, shaking breath of content.

WINONA
And you will take Judah with you?

WARREN
Certainly. You shall not be separated.

WINONA
I will go with you to your home gladly. I know you never break your word--never.

PAGE: 10

PANEL: The mainland jetty. Warren in the boat with Judah at the oars; he turns and waves his hat to a small figure on the island's ridge.

PANEL: Winona on the ridge in her blue dress, a tiny figure watching, one hand up.

PANEL: When the boat is no longer visible she throws herself on the new grave, shoulders shaking, one hand in the grass.

CAPTION: One month from that day.

EXT. GRAND ISLAND HOTEL - AFTERNOON

PANEL: The hotel's front. Warren rides in on a fresh horse, bright and smiling, his saddle-bags over the pommel. 'Tavius takes the bridle. Maybee at the door with a face like a closed shutter.

WARREN
Well, Mr. Maybee. How are you, and how are my island proteges? I'll row over after dinner and surprise them.

PAGE: 11 (TURN)

PANEL: Maybee takes him by the arm and walks him upstairs; neither speaks. Warren's smile fades one step at a time.

MAYBEE
Come with me, Mr. Maxwell. I have something to tell you.

PANEL: The room. Maybee turns, ashamed, with his hat in both hands.

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
It's a sorry tale, sir; and in all my life I never befo' felt ashamed of bein' an American citizen.

PANEL: Maybee, bitter, counting on his fingers.

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
The day after you lef' thar was a claim put in by two men who had been stoppin' roun' hyar fer a month or more.

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
Nigger traders from Missouri, sir.

PANEL: Warren, protesting, a step forward.

WARREN
But how could they take the children? They were both born free. It was an illegal proceeding.

MAYBEE
The child follows the condition of the mother. That's the law.

PANEL: Warren stops. The light has changed on his face; a hand goes to his mouth.

WARREN
My God, Mr. Maybee. Where is she now, the poor, pretty child?

PAGE: 12 (SPLASH)

PANEL: Full-page splash, a golden Missouri noon. Magnolia Farm seen from the cotton fields: a rambling two-storey house wrapped in piazzas and roses, honeysuckle over the wooden stables, a high strong fence around four acres, and beyond the house a thousand acres of cotton running to the muddy Missouri. Magnolias in bloom everywhere. In the near field, small black figures bend in the cotton rows.

CAPTION: Down on a Missouri plantation, held as a slave.

CAPTION: A few miles out from Kansas City, early in the spring of 1856.

PAGE: 13

EXT. MAGNOLIA FARM, BACK PIAZZA - NOON

PANEL: Colonel Titus on the back piazza in the shade of great pines, smoking his pipe: a tall, powerful man with dark hair and a beard fast whitening, deep-set eyes that do not look one squarely in the face. Beside him in a rolling chair sits his daughter Lillian with an open book.

CAPTION: This was "Magnolia Farm," the home of Colonel Titus.

CAPTION: An Englishman by birth, he had invested his small fortune in a plantation and many slaves.

PANEL: Inset flashback: a St. Joseph, Missouri square, 1854, the Colonel on a wagon bed before a roaring crowd of men with rifles and bowie knives, one fist in the air.

COLONEL TITUS
Vote at the point of the bowie-knife and revolver.

COLONEL TITUS (CONT'D)
It is enough that the slave-holding interest wills it, from which there is no appeal.

PANEL: Lillian, blue-eyed, looking out over the fields at the cotton hands, hand on her book.

CAPTION: His daughter's affliction was a great cross to the Colonel.

PANEL: The Colonel in profile with the smoke curling; a folded letter in his pocket, a London crest on the envelope, just visible.

CAPTION: There was a letter lying snugly in his pocket, from a firm in London, that promised much, if----

PAGE: 14

PANEL: Through an open window, the hall: MRS. THOMSON, a woman with a pinched, pious mouth, grips TENNIE, a young house-girl in an apron, by the arm.

MRS. THOMSON
What's the matter with you to-day? What are you moping about? Is it Luke?

TENNIE
Luke been conjured.

PANEL: Lillian, on the porch, to the Colonel, resigned.

LILLIAN
There's Mrs. Thomson scolding Tennie again.

PANEL: Tennie in tears, hands twisted in her apron.

TENNIE
Dat yaller huzzy, Clorinder; she conjured Luke till he gone plum wil' over her.

PANEL: Mrs. Thomson, one finger in the air, hair out of its pins, enraged and pious at once.

MRS. THOMSON
Hush your mouth!

MRS. THOMSON (CONT'D)
Get the cowhide and follow me to the cellar, and I'll whip you well for aggravating me as you have to-day.

PANEL: A cellar door closing behind the two women, a thread of light; the Colonel on the porch drawing on his pipe, unmoved.

MRS. THOMSON (V.O.)
It's a deal harder for me than it is for you.

SFX: THWAP

PAGE: 15

PANEL: Lillian, to herself, watching her father stoop slightly in his chair; his gray head and bent back.

LILLIAN
His hair is right gray, and he is beginning to stoop. Oh, dear, if I'd only been a boy.

LILLIAN (CONT'D)
Maybe I could have helped him.

PANEL: Winona comes onto the porch with a goblet on a waiter: the ruby julep, a small forest of mint. Sixteen now, in a close-fitting cotton gown; her hair short, cropped in dark rings; the soft eyes wary, her bearing like a young princess.

LILLIAN (CONT'D)
Here's Winona with your julep.

PANEL: The Colonel lifts the glass, smiling gallantly; Winona kneels to refill his pipe and holds the match.

COLONEL TITUS
Your health, my daughter!

PANEL: Winona watching the family scene with bent brows; behind her eyes a flash of the island, her father on the beach.

CAPTION: She had passed from childhood to womanhood in two years of captivity.

PANEL: A lesson in the parlor: Lillian with a book, Winona standing at her shoulder to read along; at a piano in the corner Winona's fingers on the keys, Thomson in the doorway with his arms folded.

THOMSON
It'll pay ten dollars for every one invested.

PAGE: 16

PANEL: A corral at the end of the stable range. JUDAH on a rail, a lion of a man now, with an assistant overseer's cap and a coiled rope on his shoulder, watching a stable boy work a horse. Stern, silent; he has not smiled in the whole page.

CAPTION: With Judah it was different; he was made assistant overseer, because of his intelligence and his enormous strength.

CAPTION: A stern, silent man who apparently had never known boyhood.

PANEL: Thomson on the stable steps, thin lips, hollow cheeks, coal-black moustache, writing in a ledger.

CAPTION: There was no greater scoundrel in Missouri than Bill Thomson.

PANEL: A drilling ground in a field behind the farm: forty men in line with hatchets and rifles, Thomson in front on a horse, barking orders, a ragged flag.

CAPTION: He was organizing and drilling companies of men to burn and loot the infant territory.

PANEL: Close on Judah's face as he watches the drill from the rail, his own eyes lowered, his thoughts nowhere near it.

CAPTION: Into this man's hands Judah was given body and soul.

PAGE: 17

PANEL: A horse in a stable stall, ears back, eyes rolling, teeth bared, throwing its weight against the door. A half-dozen gamblers and sporting men line the rail outside, hands in belts. Thomson is among them, shaking his head.

THOMSON
Well, boys, I reckon it ain't no use; the ugly beast's beat me, and I lose the bet.

PANEL: Close on Thomson, spreading his hands, grimly cheerful.

THOMSON (CONT'D)
I'm a done coon this day, an' the hoss will have to be shot. I invite you all to stop to the shootin' party.

PANEL: Judah steps into the circle, touching his hat respectfully; the whole group turns and stares.

JUDAH
Don't shoot him yet, sir; I can tame that horse and win your bet for you.

PANEL: Thomson recoils from the boy, then recovers, sneering.

THOMSON
You go near the beast!

THOMSON (CONT'D)
He'd throw you and kill you or trample you to death, an' I'd be just fifteen hundred dollars more out of pocket.

PANEL: Colonel Titus strides into the circle with a commanding gesture, white-bearded and gentlemanly, hat in hand.

COLONEL TITUS
Let him try, Thomson, for the honor of the farm. I believe he can do it. I'll stand the loss if there is any.

PAGE: 18

PANEL: Judah steps up to the half-door and the horse's head comes over it, teeth bared. Judah does not move or tremble.

SAM
Take car', Jude. He'll put his head out an' bite.

PANEL: Close on Judah's eyes.

PANEL: Close on the horse's eye: huge, rolling, white-rimmed.

PANEL: The same two eyes, nearer. The horse blinks.

PANEL: The horse, trembling, stepping back, flecks of foam on its flank, Judah immovable as a statue with the sun on his shoulders.

CAPTION: Judah concentrated all the strength of his will in the gaze that he fixed upon the horse.

PANEL: Half an hour later: Judah's hand coming out with a lump of sugar; the horse's lips taking it.

JUDAH
Sugar, Sam.

PAGE: 19 (SPLASH)

PANEL: Full-page splash: Judah in the saddle of a rearing, bucking horse in the middle of the four-acre enclosure, heels driven into its flanks, the dust cloud around them, whip hand high. A ring of men on the fence, hats flying, the Colonel with his hat thrown high in the air. One man in the crowd mid-shout, one man, Thomson, standing very still at the rail with his face a grin of malice and fury.

SFX: WHA-HOOOEY

CAPTION: Rising in his stirrups, Judah converted the four acres of enclosure into a circus-arena, in the true style of reckless Indian riding on the Western plains.

PAGE: 20

PANEL: Beaten, trembling and flecked with foam, the horse follows Judah quietly back into the stable; men reach up to clap his back.

MAN IN THE CROWD
Hurrah for the nigger! He's beat the hoss into the middle o' nex' week!

PANEL: A man at the Colonel's elbow whispers, looking at Thomson.

MAN IN THE CROWD (CONT'D)
I wudn't be in that ar nigger's shoes, not fer no money. Bill's mad 'cause he's beat the hoss.

PANEL: Thomson on the stable steps, alone, watching Judah lead the horse out. A thin smile that doesn't reach his eyes.

THOMSON
Money won't buy him till I'm through with him.

PANEL: A week later. Judah on a road riding at an easy walk, a folded note in his hand; the Kansas City skyline small and distant. The Colonel's voice in a caption.

CAPTION: Because of his daughter's feelings slaves were never whipped on the plantation, but were sent to the slave prison in the city.

PAGE: 21 (TURN)

EXT. KANSAS CITY SLAVE PRISON - DAY

PANEL: Brick walls and a barred bell gate. Judah hands the note through; the man at the gate reads it and his face changes.

PANEL: The man at the bell gate, calling to a burly Negro behind him, quietly.

GATEKEEPER
Pete, take this nigger, and strap him down upon the stretcher; get him ready for business.

PANEL: Judah backing away, horrified, hands up.

JUDAH
What are you going to do to me?

PANEL: The gatekeeper, flat voice, unbuckling his coat.

GATEKEEPER
You'll know d----d quick! Strip yourself. I'm going to tear your black skin.

PANEL: Judah fights madly, three sturdy men on him, rope around his wrists.

PANEL: The whole group, the men dragging him toward a dark shed; Judah's feet furrowing the dirt.

PAGE: 22

INT. THE SLAVE PRISON, THE SHED - DAY

PANEL: A cross-beam in a shed; Judah strung up by his thumbs, naked to the waist, face to the wall; the lash drawn back.

SFX: SHHH-WAP

PANEL: Close on Judah's face as the lash lands; his mouth is set, eyes shut, silent.

CAPTION: He had learned his lesson of endurance in the schools of the Indian stoic.

PANEL: Thomson strolls in with a cigar, hands in pockets, looks the work over.

THOMSON
Very good. Let him breathe a minute, boys, then ten more.

PANEL: Thomson leans in to Judah's ear, almost gently.

THOMSON (CONT'D)
You've got to learn to say "massa." It don't matter what you can do nor how much you know,

THOMSON (CONT'D)
you ain't one grain better than any other nigger on the plantation.

PANEL: The whipper, with the whip trailing, to Thomson, impressed.

WHIPPER
Got grit. Ain't whimpered.

PANEL: Judah's back, a tangle of marks; his hands in the cords; his eyes open now, black, fixed on nothing.

THOMSON (O.P.)
Now, once more, boys. Make it a dozen and smart ones to wind up with.

PAGE: 23

PANEL: The last page: a close horizontal strip of Judah's face against the wall, silent, with the shadow of the lash across it.

CAPTION: All this had happened in the first year of captivity, and since that time Judah had apparently learned his place.

PANEL: Same frame, tighter. Judah's open eye.

PANEL: Same frame, tighter still. A single bead of blood from the thumb corner runs down his wrist. The eye does not blink.
